---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";

const events = [
  ["QUEUE_FULL", "WARN", "An emit was dropped: the signal's queue reached capacity. count = items dropped."],
  ["RETRY_ARMED", "WARN", "A transient failure scheduled a retry. attempt / max_retries / delay_ms; server_driven when Retry-After set the floor."],
  ["BATCH_SENT", "DEBUG", "A batch was accepted (2xx). http_status and count included."],
  ["ITEMS_DROPPED", "ERROR", "Permanent loss — max retries exhausted (drop_reason MAX_RETRIES) or a permanent 4xx (HTTP_STATUS)."],
  ["PARTIAL_SUCCESS", "WARN", "The collector accepted the batch but rejected some items (200 + PartialSuccess body): rejected count and the server's message."],
  ["SYNC_FLUSH_FAILED", "ERROR", "The synchronous metric/log flush path could not deliver."],
];
---
<Docs title="Diagnostics & delivery — otlp-c docs">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-50">Diagnostics &amp; delivery</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-slate-400">
    The library never prints. Every diagnostic is a struct —
    <code class="rounded bg-ink-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-teal-300">otlp_event_t</code> —
    delivered to your callback with the signal id, counts, HTTP
    status, retry timing, and drop reason. A second optional callback
    receives the same event rendered as a human string; the two views
    derive from one model and cannot diverge.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-100">The event codes</h2>
  <div class="mt-4 space-y-2">
    {events.map(([code, level, desc]) => (
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-ink-700 bg-ink-900/60 p-4">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-teal-300">{code}</code>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-ink-700 px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[10px] text-slate-400">{level}</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-slate-400">{desc}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-100">Delivery semantics</h2>
  <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-ink-700 bg-ink-900/60 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-100">Retry</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        Full-jitter exponential backoff on 429/5xx/network errors.
        Retry-After is honored as a floor, clamped by your
        backoff_max_ms — a hostile server cannot stall exports.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-ink-700 bg-ink-900/60 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-100">PartialSuccess</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        A 200 can still report server-side loss. The response body is
        decoded (reference-validated) and surfaced as an event plus
        per-signal <code class="font-mono text-xs text-teal-300">rejected_*</code> stats.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-ink-700 bg-ink-900/60 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-100">Stats</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
        <code class="font-mono text-xs text-teal-300">otlp_exporter_get_stats()</code> —
        thread-safe — reports emitted/sent/dropped per signal and
        2xx/4xx/5xx/network counters. The invariant
        <em>emitted = sent + dropped</em> holds even under OOM.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-100">One event, two callbacks</h2>
  <pre class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-ink-700 bg-ink-950 p-5 font-mono text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300"><code>{`/* Structured — no string parsing */
otlp_exporter_set_event_logger(exp, on_event, ctx);

/* Human line — derived from the same otlp_event_t */
otlp_exporter_set_logger(exp, on_line, ctx);

/* Both NULL by default: zero overhead when uninstalled */`}</code></pre>
</Docs>
